KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20 --no-unwinding-assertions
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
After the strip-tag fix in cpp_instantiate_template.cpp made
constexpr-eval / eager-convert work for namespaced template
methods, std::optional<int> o = 42; now triggers a much deeper
class-instantiation cascade through libstdc++'s
_Optional_base, _Optional_payload_base,
is_trivially_destructible_v and the family of constexpr
operator bool / star / arrow / value accessors.  The resulting
goto program has many more functions to model than before
(symbol table grows by roughly 50 percent) and the BMC solver
runs out of memory at solve time on the CI box at the 6 GB
ulimit.  Increasing the limit doesn't help; the problem is the
per-symbol bookkeeping cost of the additional functions that
are now generated even though most of them are unused at
runtime.

Reclassified as KNOWNBUG until the constexpr-eval path can be
made cheaper or the goto-conversion path can drop unused
constexpr-only methods.
